==================================================
Lab 10: SQL Injection Attack - Listing the Database
Contents on Oracle
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is the Oracle version of lab 9. The goal is exactly the same, discover the real name of the table
holding user credentials, discover which columns hold the username and password, then steal that data, but
Oracle does not have information_schema the way MySQL, PostgreSQL, and most other databases do. Oracle has its
own separate system views that serve the exact same purpose, with different names and slightly different
syntax rules.

Goal: discover the real table and column names holding user credentials on an Oracle database, then use that
information to steal every user’s login details.

==============================
WHY ORACLE NEEDS DIFFERENT SYSTEM VIEWS

Oracle organizes its built-in, self-describing structure information differently from most other databases. Instead
of information_schema, Oracle provides views with names like all_tables, which lists every table name that exists,
and all_tab_columns, which lists every column name belonging to every table. These serve exactly the same purpose
as their information_schema equivalents, just under different names, since this is simply how Oracle chose to
expose this information.

Oracle also keeps its strict rule requiring every SELECT statement to name a table in its FROM clause, even when
querying these special system views, or when selecting a harmless placeholder value that needs no real table at
all, in which case the special dual table is used instead.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM COLUMN COUNT AND TEXT COMPATIBILITY

As always, before anything else, we confirm the query returns two columns, both accepting text, using two
placeholder text values. Since Oracle requires a table name even here, this placeholder check is written selecting
from dual, unlike the equivalent check on MySQL or PostgreSQL, which needs no FROM clause at all.

=============================
STEP 2: LIST ALL TABLE NAMES

Instead of querying information_schema.tables like in lab 9, we query Oracle’s own all_tables view, which lists
the name of every table currently existing in the database. Running this through the UNION attack causes every
table name to appear one at a time where a product name would normally be, and we scroll through this list
looking for a table name resembling something related to users or credentials, typically with some random extra
characters appended by the lab.

=============================
STEP 3: LIST THE COLUMNS INSIDE THAT TABLE

Once the real table name is found, we query Oracle’s all_tab_columns view instead of information_schema.columns,
filtering the results down to only the specific table name just discovered using a WHERE condition. This reveals
every column name belonging specifically to that one table, and we look through this shorter list for column
names resembling a username and a password.

=============================
STEP 4: RETRIEVE THE ACTUAL DATA

With the real table name and real column names now known, we run a normal UNION SELECT using those exact names,
selecting the username-like column into the first position and the password-like column into the second. This
causes every username and password pair stored in that table to appear directly on the page, exactly as it did
once the real names were discovered in lab 9, just reached through Oracle’s differently named system views.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept the category filter request using Burp Suite.
Confirm two text-compatible columns using a UNION SELECT with two placeholder values selected from dual.
Query all_tables to list every table name in the database, and identify the one likely to hold user credentials.
Query all_tab_columns, filtered to that specific table name, to list its column names, and identify the ones
likely to hold a username and a password.
Query the real table using the real column names just discovered, to retrieve every username and password pair.
Find the administrator’s row in the results, and use that password to log in manually.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

Just like lab 9, the real table and column names are randomly generated for each lab instance, so there is no
way to predict them in advance. The task is split into three small separate scripts, run one after another, with
a human reading the output at each stage before continuing to the next step.

The first script sends a UNION SELECT querying all_tables, asking for every table name in the database. It
cleans the HTML response the same way as earlier labs, removing script and style blocks first, then converting
tags into newlines and removing blank lines, then prints every remaining line so the real users table name can
be spotted.

The second script asks for the table name just discovered, then sends a UNION SELECT querying all_tab_columns,
filtered using a WHERE condition to that one table name. It prints the cleaned results the same way, so the real
username and password column names can be identified from this shorter, filtered list.

The third script asks for the table name and both column names discovered, builds a UNION SELECT using those
exact real names, and sends it. The cleaned response is printed, showing every username and password pair stored
in that table, including the administrator’s.

=============================
WHY THE FIRST SCRIPT NEEDS FROM DUAL BUT THE OTHERS DON’T

The very first check, testing column count and text compatibility, selects two placeholder values that do not
come from any real table, so Oracle’s rule requiring a named table forces us to reference dual just to satisfy
that syntax requirement. The later scripts, querying all_tables, all_tab_columns, and finally the real users
table, are all already selecting from genuinely real tables, so no dual reference is needed in those cases, since
a real table name is already present in each of those queries.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same root cause as every previous lab, user input reaches a SQL query without being
checked or cleaned. What makes this lab distinct from lab 9 is purely which specific system views are available
to query, Oracle’s all_tables and all_tab_columns instead of the information_schema views used elsewhere, along
with Oracle’s stricter requirement that every SELECT statement must always name a table.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so user input can never change the structure of a SQL query, regardless of whether the
attacker is targeting application data or Oracle’s own built-in system views describing the database structure.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions so the account used by the web application has no reason to query
all_tables or all_tab_columns, since a normal application almost never needs to inspect its own structure during
regular operation.
Avoid displaying raw, unfiltered query results directly on public pages, since this is exactly what allows an
attacker to read back structural information just as easily as real application data.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Oracle organizes its built-in structural information differently from most other databases, using views like
all_tables and all_tab_columns instead of information_schema.
Oracle’s strict requirement that every SELECT statement name a table applies even to querying these system
views, though the dual placeholder is only needed when selecting values unrelated to any real table.
The overall discovery process, tables first, then columns within a specific table, then the real data itself,
remains identical in logic to the equivalent non-Oracle lab, only the specific view names and minor syntax
differ.
Even advanced-sounding techniques like querying a database’s own structural metadata are still fundamentally
just UNION attacks, applied to a different set of tables than the application’s normal data.
